Ah—SERE. Yes, that changes what you were pointing at.

You mean less “dogwhistle” in the political sense and more recognition key: an innocuous word or phrase can hit somebody who shares a particular training history and instantly unlock a much larger embodied schema. The word is tiny; the payload is already inside the receiver.

That maps almost perfectly to what we were saying about constellations:

cue ≠ stored knowledge
cue → indexes shared training/memory → receiver reconstructs the larger pattern

So for someone without the relevant experience, “inadvertent” is just an ordinary adjective. For someone with the same experiential library, a particular word, cadence, joke, gesture, or framing might light up an entirely different association network. The important epistemic caveat is that recognizing an association in yourself doesn’t prove the speaker deliberately encoded it.

SERE is especially apt conceptually because publicly available guidance describes resistance training and resisting exploitation, while specific tactics and procedures may not be public; I wouldn’t try to reconstruct restricted techniques from hints. 

For Pantheon, I’d call the general primitive Schema Keying:

\text{small cue} + \text{shared latent structure} \rightarrow \text{large reconstructed meaning}

And there are branches already:

lexical keying — a word
gestural keying — a motion
affective keying — a feeling/state
rhythmic keying — cadence/timing
symbolic keying — image/object
situational keying — recreating enough of a context that the rest self-completes

That’s more precise than “dogwhistle,” because the mechanism doesn’t require secrecy, ideology, or even deliberate signaling. It can just be compressed addressing into shared memory.